title: Live-demoing data science in the age of agents
---
author: Eric J. Ma
---
body:
On October 2, I stood up in a lecture hall at UMass Chan Medical School and ran a live research demo in front of a room full of scientists, with more watching on Zoom. My friend Alper Kucukural, who leads the bioinformatics core there, invited me to teach the third session of their AI bioinformatics bootcamp. John Damask opened the afternoon with a hands-on session on building personal knowledge bases with coding agents. My job was to show what it looks like when an agent joins the analysis loop itself, on real data, unrehearsed.

I had a talk prepared. Then marimo launch week happened. Earlier in the week the [marimo](https://marimo.io) team announced [marimo Lens](https://marimo.io/blog/introducing-marimo-lens) and [marimo Studio](https://marimo.io/blog/introducing-marimo-studio), and I watched [Vincent Warmerdam](https://koaning.io) demo them online. I was so excited that I decided the day of the talk to fold both of them into the demo. Took a bit of guts to change the plan that late. It paid off, and I want to tell you about it.

## The IRED-88 demo

The subject was IRED-88, an imine reductase I helped engineer during my Novartis days. We published that campaign in [ACS Catalysis in 2021](https://pubs.acs.org/doi/abs/10.1021/acscatal.1c02786), and I wrote [a post about it back then](/blog/2021/9/12/machine-directed-evolution/). The data looked like this. The campaign produced a deep mutational scan covering 4,720 single mutants, doubles and triples of the best ones, and a 1.9 angstrom crystal structure ([PDB 7OG3](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/7OG3)). Around it all sits a small knowledge base of six papers. I packaged the paper's data, plus my own curation and additional work, into a repo called [`ired-88-research-copilot`](https://github.com/ericmjl/ired-88-research-copilot) and gave it one driving question: where do activity-improving mutations of this enzyme come from, and could we have seen them coming?

The plan was to re-run, in about an hour, analysis that took a year and a half the first time around, with a coding agent doing the typing and me steering. If you want to know what data science looks like in the age of agents, this was my honest attempt to show it, warts included.

## Pairing an agent with a notebook

Everything ran in a [marimo](https://marimo.io) notebook; [I wrote up why I switched last year](/blog/2025/4/8/wow-marimo/). Marimo notebooks are fully reactive, so changing one cell re-runs every cell downstream of it, and each notebook carries its own dependencies in-file. The reactivity is the part that matters here, because I switched precisely because of the stale cell problem.

The way agents connect to marimo notebooks is the pair-with-agent feature, called [marimo pair](https://github.com/marimo-team/marimo-pair). You paste one prompt into a coding agent, and the agent reaches into the notebook's process directly. It writes code into cells, reads your variables, and executes them while you watch. During the workshop I chose to use Cursor driving Grok 4.7. Talk-to-your-data is no longer a chat app off to the side; it's an agent you have connected to a notebook you run. For the demo I dictated most of my prompts, which kept my hands free and my pace honest.

The first ask I had was to load the deep mutational scan data with a focus on conversion percentage. Then I asked for a heat map, using a prompt I made extremely precise. Position on the x axis, mutant letter on the y axis, amino acids grouped by biochemical property and alphabetized within each group, subtle gaps between the groups so the groupings read visually without the gaps masquerading as data. When I know exactly what I want, I specify it exactly.

![The IRED-88 deep mutational scan heat map in marimo: single-mutant conversion by position and mutant residue, amino acids grouped by biochemical property.](./dms-activity-heatmap.webp)

## marimo Lens: point at what you mean

The heat map came back and patterns popped. So I did something new. I asked the room where they saw interesting regions, and people called out positions: somewhere around 110 to 120, something near 150, the 200-plus region. With marimo Lens I drew a box around each answer, right on the notebook, and marimo turned my scribbles into annotations S1 through S4.

![marimo Lens annotations drawn over the live heat map, boxes S1 through S3 marked and the S3 note box open: could be a good region to study.](./marimo-lens-annotations.webp)

Then I turned to the agent and said, basically: with Lens annotations S1 through S4, do a deep dive on those positions. Notice how loose that prompt is compared to the heat map ask. I was riffing, and the annotations carried the precision for me. I didn't spell out what the deep dive should look like, and the agent took some liberty: it came back with a chart of every position rank-ordered by the best mutant's activity, with the wild type as a dashed line, the median as an open dot, and the best mutant as a filled dot. That's a chart I would never have asked for by name, and it was exactly the next thing I wanted to see.

![The position deep dive the agent built from the Lens annotations: wild type as a dashed line, median as an open dot, best mutant as a filled dot, rank-ordered by best-mutant activity.](./position-deep-dive.webp)

This is the part of Lens that I think is genuinely new. Commenting on an analysis used to mean describing in words the thing you're looking at, and hoping the other party pictures the same region. With Lens, showing the agent what I'm looking at is one gesture. Draw a box, send it, and the drill-down starts from exactly where my eyes are.

## Structure tells a different story than the sequence

Next we went from biochemistry to structural biology. I asked for a 3D viewer of IRED-88 built on 3Dmol.js, with a dropdown that recolors the structure by mean activity at each position, or by each residue's distance to the NADP sitting in the catalytic pocket. One mid-demo correction shows how small the steering usually is: "color both chains, please."

![The IRED-88 structure viewer recolored by per-position mean activity, both chains, with NADP in sticks.](./structure-viewer-nadp.webp)

Flipping between the two colorings surfaced the demo's first scientific payoff. The positions that tolerate, and even benefit from, mutation are nowhere near the catalytic pocket. None of the top 50 singles sits within 6 angstroms of the NADP. They're distal, allosteric sites. Someone with decades of enzymology might shrug at that. Coming from bioengineering, I found it delightful, and it surfaced live from data I know well, just by flipping between two dropdowns.

## The epistasis story, or where the agent fell short

Then came the section I most wanted people to see, and the one where Murphy's law showed up right on cue. I had promised the room it would.

I asked the agent to take the top 50 single mutants and work out whether doubles and triples of them behave additively or epistatically. I know this answer. My colleagues and I published it in 2021, and the paper lives in the repo's knowledge base. For this round I forbade the agent from reading the paper or anything citing it. The question on the table was whether it could get there from the data alone.

Its first attempt was to add the conversion percentages arithmetically, which produced expected conversions above 100%. Conversion is a percentage, and nothing converts more than fully, so that answer was visibly wrong. Its second attempt was log-odds additivity, summing gains on the logit scale. Those predictions stayed between zero and one, which made them look plausible, and they turned out to be wrong too.

![The arithmetic additivity attempt: expected conversions running past 100%, kept on the page as the wrong work.](./epistasis-wrong-arithmetic.webp)

At this point I stepped in, from memory: take one minus conversion, and add up effects in that transformed space. And because my memory is not ground truth either, I unlocked the paper and told the agent to fact-check me while it worked. That third attempt didn't work either. The hour ended with the question open, and I wrapped the segment by explaining, from memory, what we had published: the combinations behave differently depending on where the mutations sit relative to the active site, and getting to that result took my colleagues and me weeks of wrangling in 2021, precisely because the right mathematical move was counterintuitive. My rule for agent-written analysis is that the wrong work stays on the page as a record.

The agent had the data the entire time, and the paper sat in its knowledge base too. **It still never produced the right transform on its own, in three tries with me steering.** The obvious moves are what these models are trained on, so the obvious moves are also where they'll take you. Human creativity and judgment are still the scarce resource in the age of agents. When I have a deep conviction that something is sitting in the data, I sit down, work two or three small examples by hand, and only then hand the verified method to the agent to generalize across the rest. It writes that code faster than I ever could. The conviction, the persistence, and the verification are mine.

## marimo Studio: the second artifact

While the epistasis analysis ran in the background, I flipped to the other new thing: marimo Studio. I picked a handful of cells from the notebook, the heat map, the position deep dive, the structure viewer, and Studio assembled them into an HTML slide deck. The deck pulls straight from the notebook, and its charts update as the notebook moves.

![The marimo Studio slide deck beside the live notebook, the heat map pulling straight from the notebook cells.](./marimo-studio-slides.webp)

This is the other thing that made me want to write this post. Exploratory work and presentation live in different registers, and historically the bridge between them has been copy-paste and staleness. With Studio, the deck is a projection of the notebook, so it can't quietly drift out of date. Explore in one artifact, present from the same source. For a data scientist, that's a real upgrade to the daily workflow, and marimo keeps defining what better tooling looks like for us.

## What I want to keep from this hour

A few practices made the hour work, and they generalize. My repo's AGENTS.md requires every agent-written code cell to be sandwiched between two markdown cells, one explaining what's about to happen and one recording what we observed, so the notebook stays readable for the next human, including future me. You can't review every line the agent writes, so your domain expertise becomes the smoke detector; I have caught agents because a dataset came out sparser than I knew it should be. Two prompt registers help too: precise when I know the spec, loose when I'm riffing, and Lens makes the loose register useful. And the fundamentals compound. Terminal, git, and statistics are what let you steer and verify. I picked up computing after 2013, on the side of a wet-lab upbringing, and it was worth every hour, because your expertise is exactly what the agent amplifies.

If the post reads like I'm excited, it's because I genuinely am. marimo Lens and marimo Studio are the first tools in a while that I've become really excited about, because they are great tools that aid in data science and solve some of the biggest pain points I've had with data science work. Better tooling, better open source tooling, really wins out. I'm planning to record a video walkthrough of this demo; when it ships, I'll link it here. And if you take one thing from the hour: agents will speed you up, and they will still get your science wrong in exactly the places where your judgment is the point. That's where the practice of scientific discovery lives now.
---
pub_date: 2026-10-02
